 object, an unknown `op`, a missing or mistyped `op`, `path`, `from` or `value`), or when an operation fails
 (a missing target, an out-of-range index, a failed `test`, a `move` into its own child); no partial result
 is returned. Keys such as `__proto__` are plain members and never reach the prototype chain.
+
+## JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from "./src/index.js";
+
+applyMergePatch({ a: "b", c: { d: "e" } }, { a: "z", c: { d: null } }); // { a: "z", c: {} }
+applyMergePatch({ a: "b" }, ["c"]); // ["c"]
+```
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies a merge patch as specified in RFC 7386 section 2 and returns the
+merged document as a new value; `target` and `patch` are not mutated and the result shares no structure
+with them. A patch that is not an object (including an array or `null`) replaces the target as a whole;
+an object patch is merged into the target member by member (into `{}` if the target is not an object),
+a `null` member removing that member from the target. Arrays are replaced, never merged, so a `null`
+inside an array is kept; as the RFC notes, `null` cannot be set as a member value. It does not throw.
+Keys such as `__proto__` are plain members and never reach the prototype chain.
